          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"in an udumbara fruit; \u0909\u0926\u0941\u092e\u094d\u092c\u0930\u092e\u0936\u0915, \u0909\u0926\u0941\u092e\u094d\u092c\u0930\u0915\u0943\u092e\u093f &c. are proverbial\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"expressions like \u0915\u0942\u092a\u092e\u0923\u094d\u0921\u0942\u0915 meaning \u2018any useless person.\u2019\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The two Devanagari expressions on the first line are heavily inked and uncertain.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 25. \u0917\u093e\u0932\u093f\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0926\u093e\u0928\u0903\u2014\u092e\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0930 expresses \u0928\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0926\u093e (because nothing bet-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"ter can be expected of you). \u0936\u0936\u0915\u0935\u093f\u0937\u093e\u0923\u2014see N\u00eeti. S\u2019a. sl. 5.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The first Devanagari expression is unclear.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 26. \u092e\u0939\u093e\u0930\u093e\u092e\u2014\u0906\u0930\u093e\u092e means a garden, but more probably\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"here \u2018delight, pleasure; his path is full of delight because he is\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"free from all desire. \u091a\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0928\u094d\u2014( 1 ) bark; ( 2 ) skin. \u0938\u0941\u0916\u0948\u0903 &c.\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"Mr. Telang says\u2014\u201cThe result is the same with what are called\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"pleasures or with sorrows; i. e. whether I am in wordly affluence\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"or otherwise, I get food and clothing in one case as in the other.\u201d\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"But \u0938\u0941\u0916 and \u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916 here seem to refer to the two courses of life\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"marked out, viz. that of happily living on alms and worshipping\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"S\u2019iva or that of living a miserable life of sycophany and hu-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"mouring rich men. \u0938\u0926\u0943\u0936\u2014should better be rendered by \u2018similar\u2019\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"than by \u2018the same\u2019. The result, viz. obtaining food and clothing\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"is similar in both cases but not the same as the one is full of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"happiness, the other attended by misery. \u0924\u094d\u0930\u093f\u0928\u0947\u0924\u094d\u0930, \u00b0\u092e\u0939\u0940\u092d\u0943\u0924\u094d\u2014mark\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":15,\"text\":\"the contrast suggested by these words.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Devanagari words, especially on lines 4 and 14, are unclear.\"},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 27. \u0915\u0930\u091f\u093f\u0928\u0903\u2014i. e. elephants belonging to the enemy.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"The meaning of the first line is-We did not distinguish our-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"selves as successful warriors. Cf. l. 2. Sl. 47. \u0928\u093f\u0924\u092e\u094d\u092c\u00b0-\u0928\u093f\u092c\u0926\u094d\u0927\u092b\u0932\u0915\u0902\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"v. l. the boardlike i. e. expansive and fleshy or well-rounded\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"(\u0928\u093f\u092c\u0926\u094d\u0927) hips. \u091d\u0923\u091d\u093e\u0919\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930-a tautological expression; \u091d\u0923 and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"\u091d\u0902\u0915\u093e\u0930 meaning the same thing. For the idea comp. \u0938\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u0947 \u0938\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u0947\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"\u092e\u0941\u0916\u0930\u0915\u0915\u0941\u092d\u094b \u091d\u093e\u0902\u0915\u0943\u0924\u0948\u0930\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u091d\u0930\u093e\u0923\u093e\u092e\u094d \u2019 Uttar. II. 14. \u0935\u092f\u0903\u2014the reading \u0935\u092f\u0903\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"makes no sense. The fourth line is the same as that of sl. 48.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Several Sanskrit quotations are indistinct, especially on lines 3 and 5\u20137.\"},\"r6\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Additional Sl.\u2014\u092c\u0932\u0940\u2014A demon; his story is too well known to\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"the Hindu students to require any notice here; see A. Dictionary.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0927\u092f\u0903\u2014i. e. the diseases in the world. We did not free the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"people from the diseases humanity is heir to. Mr. Telang who\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"reads the last line as \u00b0\u092e\u093e\u0928\u0917\u0923\u0928\u093e \u092e\u093f\u0925\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0935\u0939\u0928\u094d \u0932\u091c\u094d\u091c\u0924\u0947 remarks.\u2014\u2018The\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"stanza occurs in the Mah\u00e2n\u00e2taka. The last line is not metrical\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"as it stands. In the Mah\u00e2n\u00e2taka it runs thus\u2014\u0927\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0903 \u0938\u093e \u092a\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0937\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\",\"end\":\"join\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit readings on lines 5 and 7 are indistinct; the last word continues on the next page.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 84 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\",\"u6\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"25\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"26\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"27\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r5\"]},{\"id\":\"u6\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":\"Additional Sl.\",\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r6\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]}],\"relations\":[]}"
        }
